  whenToUse: 'The single review path for any diff/PR before commit or merge. The engine itself always recalls the active decisions, questions and project rules for the paths of the diff through the craft:memory skill; priorDecisions is optional and ADDS to that recall, never replaces it (merged by id, the recalled record kept on a clash; an empty list adds nothing; a passed record may leave out id when it carries kind, title and scope, the id then derived from them by the memory skill rule), and the report names both parts. To post findings on a PR pass comment — never post findings by hand: only comments from the engine carry the marker that ties a later rejection to its finding. With comment only defects are posted: a doubtful finding (not confirmed, raised by the compat, intent or api-boundary lens, about rollback, rollout, old replicas, migration or a breaking change, or outside the changed lines) is listed under Doubtful — for the reviewer, not posted, numbered as the run record keeps it; show that list to the human and, for each he chooses, post it as a question or a defect only through the craft:addressing-findings posting script. Auto-detects language; pin with args.languages (e.g. ["rust"] or ["nix"]). Scales depth to the diff automatically. To review ANOTHER repository pass repo=<absolute path> — without it every git command runs in the checkout the session itself sits in; path= is a repo-relative pathspec, NOT a way to select the repo. The performance / api-idioms / api-boundary lenses are an OPTIONAL pass that is OFF by default — request it with optional=true (or optional=performance,api-boundary); every report names what it skipped. priorDecisions — ONLY inside an object argument, {priorDecisions: [<decision, question and rule records the launcher holds beyond what the engine recalls, such as rejections read from PR threads>]}; as a string (key=value or JSON text) it is refused and nothing of it applied, and in a key=value string nothing after it is read as an option — sets aside a finding the project already rejected — listed under Rejected before with who, when, why and a link, never dropped — unless it is Critical/High or its scope changed since the commit of the decision; a finding an active question (a deferred finding) answers is listed under Known and deferred by the same rules; a finding a judge puts under an active project rule (a stance on a whole class of findings) is listed under Covered by project rules, out of the verdict and never posted, unless it is Critical; a malformed value applies nothing and is named in the report. depth=light is a cheaper pass: one round of the diff-scoped lenses only (no negative-space or invariants; tests and intent still run whenever the diff changes production code, reconciler and failure-windows on controller code, compat on a public contract path), no completeness critic, and only Medium and above verified; its verdict and report say light pass and name what it skipped, and the run record carries depth (default depth=full, the review as it was). The engine accepts only depth=light (any other value runs full): routing a "quick" or "light" request to it belongs to the calling skill or hook. The consumer repository\'s own reviewing agents can run as extra lenses in the same run: each reviews the whole change and its findings go through the same verification and verdict. On a light pass with extraLenses absent, the engine discovers them among the .claude/agents of the project the session runs in, and only when repo= is absent or names that project or one of its worktrees (a foreign repo skips discovery and the report says so) (an agent that reviews and has no edit tool -- its tools list names none, or its disallowedTools take them all away -- told to stay read-only; the report says when none was found); a full review runs them only when named. extraLenses=[<agentType>, ...] (an object argument, e.g. {extraLenses: ["reviewer"]}) names them at either depth, and extraLenses=[] turns them off; a named agent that is unknown or fails is a coverage gap (INCOMPLETE), never a failed run; a discovered one that cannot run is reported as such, never a gap. The report names which extra lenses ran and how they were chosen. crateScope=true is an experimental rust-only mode, off by default: each code-intrinsic lens is confined to one Cargo crate and asks a live coordinator about the others, a seam pass checks the cross-crate integration and a contract lens judges each touched crate\'s public contract; the lenses run over crate batches (crates joined by a Cargo dependency together, small ones packed, a big one split by module, each batch under a volume budget of changed and dependency-signature lines, crateBatchBudget=<lines> overrides it), tests and reconciler one agent per batch, safety, errors, concurrency and maintainability one agent per batch in sections, each finding counted under its own lens (crateBatches=false gives one agent per crate and lens instead); the run record carries crateScope, the batches and a cost estimate of the plan from placeholder coefficients (costModel={fixed, perChangedLine, perCodeLine, perSigLine} overrides them), and after the run `node "<craft plugin root>/lib/crate-boundary.mjs" ~/.claude/projects/<project slug>/<session id>/subagents/workflows/wf_<run id>` (the plugin root is two directories above a craft skill\'s base directory; the run directory holds the agent-*.jsonl transcripts) lists what the confined lenses and the contract lens read outside their crate. deadlineMs=<ms> is a diagnostic knob, not a review option: it replaces the per-phase wall-clock deadline table wholesale and will kill healthy lenses if set below their real duration.',
